{"name":"Selenium","hex":"#777e7a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"selenium","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A nonmetallic chemical element (symbol Se) with an atomic number of 34, used mainly in glassmaking and pigments and as a semiconductor.","An atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/selenium"}],"entries":[{"title":"Selenium","blocks":[{"text":"Selenium is a chemical element; it has symbol Se and atomic number 34. It has various physical appearances, including a brick-red powder, a vitreous black solid, and a grey metallic-looking form. It seldom occurs in this elemental state or as pure ore compounds in Earth's crust. Selenium was discovered in 1817 by Jöns Jacob Berzelius, who noted the similarity of the new element to the previously discovered tellurium."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/SeBlackRed.jpg/330px-SeBlackRed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selenium"},{"title":"Selenium (software)","blocks":[{"text":"Selenium is an open source umbrella project for a range of tools and libraries aimed at supporting browser automation. It provides a playback tool for authoring functional tests across most modern web browsers, without the need to learn a test scripting language. It also provides a test domain-specific language (Selenese) to write tests in a number of popular programming languages, including JavaScript (Node.js), C#, Groovy, Java, Perl, PHP, Python, Ruby and Scala. Selenium runs on Windows, Linux, and macOS. It is open-source software released under the Apache License 2.0."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selenium_(software)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Selenium_trigonal.jpg/500px-Selenium_trigonal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Selenium_trigonal.jpg/500px-Selenium_trigonal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure of hexagonal (gray) selenium","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASelenium_trigonal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Selenium-dioxide-chain-3D-balls.png/500px-Selenium-dioxide-chain-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Selenium-dioxide-chain-3D-balls.png/500px-Selenium-dioxide-chain-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure of the polymer SeO2: The (pyramidal) selenium atoms are yellow.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASelenium-dioxide-chain-3D-balls.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Luna_statue.jpg/500px-Luna_statue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Luna_statue.jpg/500px-Luna_statue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Selenium is named after Selene, the Greek Goddess of the Moon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALuna_statue.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Selenium_in_sandstone_Westwater_Canyon_Section_23_Mine_Grants%2C_New_Mexico.jpg/500px-Selenium_in_sandstone_Westwater_Canyon_Section_23_Mine_Grants%2C_New_Mexico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Selenium_in_sandstone_Westwater_Canyon_Section_23_Mine_Grants%2C_New_Mexico.jpg/1280px-Selenium_in_sandstone_Westwater_Canyon_Section_23_Mine_Grants%2C_New_Mexico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co